From 2910b4136ae6c10c1aed70eef805f292fa4f2661 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Beany <24502088+beanyforever@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:40:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A search leaves the blocks it finds reading whole

Three searches read their rows from blocks_fts with snippet() or
highlight() in place of the block's fields: the full-text search's FTS
route (fullTextSearchByFTSInBox, which a keyword search takes when it
doesn't search paths and a query-syntax search always takes), the
block-ref search typed after (( (fullTextSearchRefBlockInBox), and the
highlighting of a page opened from a search (highlightByFTSInBox). Their
rows went through scanBlockRows, which stores every row in SQL's block
cache, stripping search marks from Content only. GetBlock and GetBlocks read
that cache first, so until the block changed or the cache was cleared, a
found block read cut to its snippet, or with search marks in its name,
alias, memo, tag or attributes, wherever SiYuan reads a block by id: ref
text in exports and templates, and the backlinks panel, among them.

The two routes those searches use, SelectBlocksRawStmtArgsInBox and
SelectBlocksRawStmtNoParseInBox, get NoCache twins that scan rows without
storing them, through scanBlockRowsNoCache, and the three searches use
them. Results are built from the rows returned, so nothing they show
changes. Every other read of blocks caches as before; the default keyword
search with paths, and multi-word searches, already read rows as maps.

Its Go test is TestSearchLeavesBlocksWhole, in search_cache_fts5_test.go,
on a page holding a paragraph of 7,896 characters named "needle list": a
keyword search without paths, a query-syntax search, a block-ref search,
and opening the page with the query needle, then GetBlock of the page and
the paragraph. Without the patch, on v3.8.7 with 0001 to 0008 applied, the
first two left the paragraph 515 characters long and the block-ref search
67, all three with search marks in its name, and opening the page left the
marks; without any one of the three changes, its own case fails.

Still open: SQL's block cache changes before the SQL commit, so a read in
that moment can cache the old row; and a search by SQL, or an embed block,
whose own statement selects snippet() or highlight() in place of a block's
fields still caches what it reads.
